  <!-- SECTION 1: DEEP CONCEPTUAL EXPLANATION -->
  <section>
    <h4 style="border-left: 3px solid var(--info); padding-left: 8px; margin-top: 24px; margin-bottom: 10px; color: var(--text-primary); font-weight: 600;">Deep Conceptual Explanation</h4>
    <p>The <b>Preamble</b> serves as the introductory statement of the Constitution, acting as its 'Identity Card'. It is based on the [[Objectives Resolution]] drafted and moved by [[Jawaharlal Nehru]] on December 13, 1946. It outlines the <b>source of authority</b> (the people of India), the <b>nature of the Indian State</b> (Sovereign, Socialist, Secular, Democratic, Republic), and the <b>objectives</b> of the Constitution: Justice, Liberty, Equality, and Fraternity. It embodies the fundamental values and philosophy upon which the entire legal framework of the nation is built.</p><p>A critical legal evolution occurred through the [[Kesavananda Bharati Case]] (1973), where the Supreme Court ruled that the Preamble is <b>part of the Constitution</b> and can be amended under Article 368, provided the <b>Basic Structure</b> is not destroyed. The only amendment to the Preamble took place via the [[42nd Amendment Act, 1976]], which added three new words: <b>'Socialist'</b>, <b>'Secular'</b>, and <b>'Integrity'</b>. This amendment reflected the state's commitment to a welfare society and religious neutrality during the Emergency period.</p><p>The Preamble is <b>non-justiciable</b>, meaning its provisions are not enforceable in a court of law. However, it serves as a guiding light for the judiciary when interpreting ambiguous statutes. Its significance lies in defining India as a <b>Sovereign Republic</b>, ensuring that power resides with the people and that the head of state is elected, not hereditary, thereby cementing the democratic fabric of the nation.</p>
  </section>

